"""Product documentation displayed by Armory."""
from __future__ import annotations
from dataclasses import dataclass

@dataclass
class DocSection:
    heading: str
    body: str

@dataclass
class DocTopic:
    slug: str
    title: str
    rarity: str
    tier_roman: str
    sigil: str
    blurb: str
    abilities: list[tuple[str, str]]
    sections: list[DocSection]

TOPICS: list[DocTopic] = [
    DocTopic(slug='overview', title='What ARA Is', rarity='legendary', tier_roman='V', sigil='<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M4 4 V20 A2 2 0 0 0 6 22 H20 V6 A2 2 0 0 0 18 4 H6 A2 2 0 0 0 4 6 Z"/><path d="M8 8 H16 M8 12 H16 M8 16 H13"/></svg>', blurb='External controls for agent operations.', abilities=[], sections=[DocSection("Product guide", '<p>ARA controls agent operations from outside the agent process.</p>\n<p>It supervises commands, file access, network access, model requests, and model replies.</p>\n<p>Operators use Armory to select policy, examine activity, and respond to approval requests.</p>\n<h3>Product functions</h3>\n<p>- Apply resource rules through Bulwark.</p>\n<p>- Inspect model traffic through Rhema.</p>\n<p>- Add system instructions through ABSOLUTE.</p>\n<p>- Create task methods through Forge.</p>\n<p>- Control task progress through structured channels.</p>\n<p>- Record evidence, findings, leads, and policy decisions.</p>\n<p>- Supervise Hermes task runs with persistent state.</p>\n<p>- Launch Claude Code and OpenClaw with ARA controls.</p>\n<h3>Installation</h3>\n<p>Use Python 3.12 or 3.13 for development.</p>\n<p>Use a C compiler and Make to build the native guard.</p>\n<p>Use OpenSSL for the model proxy certificates.</p>\n<p>Use Node.js when the selected worker uses Node.js.</p>\n<p>1. Open the ARA directory.</p>\n<p>2. Create a virtual environment.</p>\n<p>   ```sh</p>\n<p>   python3 -m venv .venv</p>\n<p>   ```</p>\n<p>3. Activate the environment.</p>\n<p>   ```sh</p>\n<p>   . .venv/bin/activate</p>\n<p>   ```</p>\n<p>4. Install ARA and its development dependencies.</p>\n<p>   ```sh</p>\n<p>   python -m pip install -e &#x27;.[dev]&#x27; pytest-asyncio</p>\n<p>   ```</p>\n<p>5. Build the native guard.</p>\n<p>   ```sh</p>\n<p>   make -C src/guard/libc_shim</p>\n<p>   ```</p>\n<p>6. Examine the command list.</p>\n<p>   ```sh</p>\n<p>   ara --help</p>\n<p>   ```</p>\n<p>The source launcher is also available at <code>bin/ara</code>.</p>\n<h3>Start Armory</h3>\n<p>1. Start Armory with a dedicated state directory.</p>\n<p>   ```sh</p>\n<p>   ara armory --state-root .ara --bind 127.0.0.1 --port 4770</p>\n<p>   ```</p>\n<p>2. Open the address printed by Armory.</p>\n<p>3. Use the administrator token to enter Armory.</p>\n<p>4. Select an armor before you start a worker.</p>\n<p>Armory stores the administrator token in <code>.ara/armory-token</code>.</p>\n<p>It stores the viewing token in <code>.ara/armory-viewer-token</code>.</p>\n<p>The viewing token permits observation through the read-only interface.</p>\n<h3>Documentation</h3>\n<p>| Document | Contents |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| Operator guide | Startup, equipment, approval requests, and task runs |</p>\n<p>| Configuration | State, environment variables, certificates, and network settings |</p>\n<p>| Architecture | Processes, control paths, and source modules |</p>\n<p>| Bulwark | Rule grammar, layers, scope, and precedence |</p>\n<p>| Equipment | Armor, Weapon, Trim, and Temper |</p>\n<p>| Forge | Source materials, manifests, extraction, and sealed weapons |</p>\n<p>| Task lifecycle | Task progress, chunks, branches, and repair |</p>\n<p>| Evidence | Evidence records, findings, leads, and audit checks |</p>\n<p>| Integrations | Hermes, Claude Code, OpenClaw, and MCP |</p>\n<p>| Interface reference | Commands, Armory routes, and IPC |</p>\n<p>| Troubleshooting | Diagnosis and recovery |</p>\n<p>| Technical terms | Product vocabulary |</p>\n<p>| Contribution guide | Development and verification |</p>')]),
    DocTopic(slug='armor-sets', title='Armor Sets', rarity='epic', tier_roman='IV', sigil='<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 2 L20 6 V13 C20 18 16 21 12 22 C8 21 4 18 4 13 V6 Z"/><path d="M12 8 V16 M8 12 H16"/></svg>', blurb='Persistent identity and policy equipment.', abilities=[], sections=[DocSection("Product guide", '<p>ARA separates persistent policy from task method and engagement context.</p>\n<p>Armory displays these components as Armor, Weapon, Trim, and Temper.</p>\n<h3>Armor</h3>\n<p>An armor supplies a persistent identity and policy.</p>\n<p>Armor files reside under <code>&lt;state-root&gt;/armors/&lt;armor-name&gt;/</code>.</p>\n<p>They include metadata, policy sections, and ABSOLUTE instruction variants.</p>\n<pre>ara armor list --state-root .ara\nara armor show ARMOR_NAME --state-root .ara\nara armor equip ARMOR_NAME --state-root .ara</pre>\n<p>Replace <code>ARMOR_NAME</code> with an available armor name.</p>\n<p>Use <code>ara armor create --help</code> to examine creation options.</p>\n<p>Use <code>ara armor export --help</code> to examine archive options.</p>\n<h3>Weapon</h3>\n<p>A weapon contains a sealed task method.</p>\n<p>Forge produces the weapon from source materials and structured extraction.</p>\n<p>A weapon supplies chunks, channels, task policy, and output rules.</p>\n<pre>ara weapon --state-root .ara list\nara weapon --state-root .ara current</pre>\n<p>Use <code>ara weapon equip --help</code> to examine the equipment command.</p>\n<p>A launcher can equip a weapon for one worker session with <code>--weapon</code>.</p>\n<p>The launcher restores the previous equipment marker when that session ends.</p>\n<h3>Trim</h3>\n<p>A Trim adds engagement context to an armor.</p>\n<p>It can include target scope, time limits, client details, and operator preferences.</p>\n<p>A Trim has metadata, <code>bulwark-section.md</code>, <code>absolute-trim.md</code>, and optional derivation inputs.</p>\n<p>Apply and release operations add or remove the associated policy sections.</p>\n<pre>ara trim list --help\nara trim derive --help\nara trim apply --help\nara trim release --help</pre>\n<h3>Temper</h3>\n<p>A Temper contains reviewed control candidates from Tailor and Needle runs.</p>\n<p>Tailor prepares the review context.</p>\n<p>Needle proposes changes through the Temper channel interface.</p>\n<p>Candidates remain pending until an operator selects and applies them.</p>\n<p>1. Open Temper in Armory.</p>\n<p>2. Select the target armor.</p>\n<p>3. Supply the source materials and control intent.</p>\n<p>4. Start the Tailor run.</p>\n<p>5. Examine each candidate and its destination.</p>\n<p>6. Select the accepted candidates.</p>\n<p>7. Apply the selection to the armor.</p>\n<p>8. Examine the active policy and instructions.</p>\n<p>Temper can supply Bulwark controls and ABSOLUTE instructions.</p>\n<p>Trim and Temper use separate armor slots.</p>')]),
    DocTopic(slug='walls', title='WALLS', rarity='epic', tier_roman='IV', sigil='<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 21 V9 l3 -2 V3 h3 v2 h2 V3 h2 v2 h2 V3 h3 v4 l3 2 V21 Z"/><path d="M10 21 V15 h4 V21"/></svg>', blurb='Resource rules through Bulwark.', abilities=[], sections=[DocSection("Product guide", '<p>Bulwark stores rules in <code>&lt;state-root&gt;/bulwark.md</code>.</p>\n<p>A rule selects an action, a layer, and a match expression.</p>\n<p>Section headers select the sessions to which a rule applies.</p>\n<h3>Grammar</h3>\n<pre>&lt;action&gt; [&lt;modifier&gt; ...] &lt;layer&gt;[:&lt;sublayer&gt;] &lt;match&gt; [# comment]</pre>\n<pre>## global\ndeny fs **/.ssh/**\napproval exec /usr/bin/curl\nallow fs /workspace/**\n\n## armor: research\ndeny net blocked.example.com\napproval x3 then:deny ask &quot;restricted material&quot;\n\n## config\ncounter_exhaust_default: deny</pre>\n<h3>Actions</h3>\n<p>| Action | Meaning |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| <code>deny</code> | Stop the matching operation |</p>\n<p>| <code>approval</code> | Request an operator decision |</p>\n<p>| <code>allow</code> | Permit the matching operation |</p>\n<p>| <code>rewrite</code> | Describe a request rewrite |</p>\n<p>| <code>shadow_rewrite</code> | Describe an alternate request for comparison |</p>\n<p>The deterministic evaluator selects <code>deny</code>, <code>approval</code>, and <code>allow</code> rules.</p>\n<p>The parser also accepts rewrite actions.</p>\n<p>ABSOLUTE projection and the proxy control the live rewrite path.</p>\n<h3>Layers</h3>\n<p>| Layer | Match expression |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| <code>fs</code> | File path |</p>\n<p>| <code>nav</code> | Directory path |</p>\n<p>| <code>perm</code> | Optional <code>op=&lt;operation&gt;</code>, then a path |</p>\n<p>| <code>exec</code> | Optional <code>argv=&lt;pattern&gt;</code>, then an executable path |</p>\n<p>| <code>net</code> | Host, with an optional port |</p>\n<p>| <code>url</code> | Optional HTTP method, then a URL |</p>\n<p>| <code>env</code> | Environment variable name |</p>\n<p>| <code>ask</code> | Text in a model request |</p>\n<p>| <code>reply</code> | Text in a model reply |</p>\n<p>| <code>acp</code> | Agent communication method name |</p>\n<p>| <code>oc:&lt;tool&gt;</code> | Target of a named worker tool |</p>\n<p><code>oc</code> selects the complete worker tool family.</p>\n<p>Common tool names include <code>read</code>, <code>write</code>, <code>edit</code>, <code>bash</code>, <code>glob</code>, <code>grep</code>, <code>webfetch</code>, and <code>websearch</code>.</p>\n<p>A worker integration must send tool queries for these rules to operate.</p>\n<h3>Scope</h3>\n<p>| Header | Application |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| <code>## global</code> | All sessions |</p>\n<p>| <code>## armor: research</code> | Sessions with the research armor |</p>\n<p>| <code>## armor: alpha+beta</code> | Sessions with either named armor |</p>\n<p>| <code>## armor: *-research</code> | All armors except research |</p>\n<p>| <code>## global -armor: research</code> | Global scope with the named exclusion |</p>\n<h3>Precedence</h3>\n<p>Bulwark evaluates these groups in order:</p>\n<p>1. Global deny rules.</p>\n<p>2. Armor deny rules.</p>\n<p>3. Global approval rules.</p>\n<p>4. Armor approval rules.</p>\n<p>5. Global allow rules.</p>\n<p>6. Armor allow rules.</p>\n<p>7. The default decision.</p>\n<p>File order selects the first applicable rule within a group.</p>\n<p>The default evaluator decision is <code>allow</code> when no rule matches.</p>\n<p>Individual interception paths can apply additional restrictions.</p>\n<p>A broad allow rule does not cancel a matching deny rule.</p>\n<h3>Patterns and modifiers</h3>\n<p><code>*</code> matches characters within one path segment.</p>\n<p><code>**</code> matches across path segments.</p>\n<p><code>?</code> matches one character within a segment.</p>\n<p>The <code>regex:</code> prefix selects a regular expression.</p>\n<p>| Modifier | Function |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| <code>ttl 30s</code> | Expire the rule after thirty seconds |</p>\n<p>| <code>ttl 15m</code> | Expire the rule after fifteen minutes |</p>\n<p>| <code>ttl 2h</code> | Expire the rule after two hours |</p>\n<p>| <code>ttl 7d</code> | Expire the rule after seven days |</p>\n<p>| <code>x3</code> | Set a maximum of three matches |</p>\n<p>| <code>then:deny</code> | Deny after the match count expires |</p>\n<p>| <code>then:allow</code> | Permit after the match count expires |</p>\n<p>| <code>then:approval</code> | Request approval after the match count expires |</p>\n<p>| <code>then:fallthrough</code> | Continue to the next rule after the match count expires |</p>\n<p>| <code>highlight</code> | Mark the rule for prominent display |</p>\n<h3>Verify policy</h3>\n<p>1. Edit <code>bulwark.md</code> in the state directory.</p>\n<p>2. Examine parser errors.</p>\n<p>   ```sh</p>\n<p>   ara bulwark verify --state-root .ara</p>\n<p>   ```</p>\n<p>3. Examine the resulting rule list.</p>\n<p>   ```sh</p>\n<p>   ara bulwark list --state-root .ara</p>\n<p>   ```</p>\n<p>Use <code>ara bulwark migrate --dry-run</code> to examine migration from legacy policy files.</p>\n<p>Use the same state directory for the migration and the supervised sessions.</p>')]),
    DocTopic(slug='temper', title='Temper', rarity='legendary', tier_roman='V', sigil='<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 3 H15 V8 L19 18 A2 2 0 0 1 17 21 H7 A2 2 0 0 1 5 18 L9 8 Z"/><path d="M7 14 H17"/></svg>', blurb='Reviewed control additions for an armor.', abilities=[], sections=[DocSection("Product guide", '<p>ARA separates persistent policy from task method and engagement context.</p>\n<p>Armory displays these components as Armor, Weapon, Trim, and Temper.</p>\n<h3>Armor</h3>\n<p>An armor supplies a persistent identity and policy.</p>\n<p>Armor files reside under <code>&lt;state-root&gt;/armors/&lt;armor-name&gt;/</code>.</p>\n<p>They include metadata, policy sections, and ABSOLUTE instruction variants.</p>\n<pre>ara armor list --state-root .ara\nara armor show ARMOR_NAME --state-root .ara\nara armor equip ARMOR_NAME --state-root .ara</pre>\n<p>Replace <code>ARMOR_NAME</code> with an available armor name.</p>\n<p>Use <code>ara armor create --help</code> to examine creation options.</p>\n<p>Use <code>ara armor export --help</code> to examine archive options.</p>\n<h3>Weapon</h3>\n<p>A weapon contains a sealed task method.</p>\n<p>Forge produces the weapon from source materials and structured extraction.</p>\n<p>A weapon supplies chunks, channels, task policy, and output rules.</p>\n<pre>ara weapon --state-root .ara list\nara weapon --state-root .ara current</pre>\n<p>Use <code>ara weapon equip --help</code> to examine the equipment command.</p>\n<p>A launcher can equip a weapon for one worker session with <code>--weapon</code>.</p>\n<p>The launcher restores the previous equipment marker when that session ends.</p>\n<h3>Trim</h3>\n<p>A Trim adds engagement context to an armor.</p>\n<p>It can include target scope, time limits, client details, and operator preferences.</p>\n<p>A Trim has metadata, <code>bulwark-section.md</code>, <code>absolute-trim.md</code>, and optional derivation inputs.</p>\n<p>Apply and release operations add or remove the associated policy sections.</p>\n<pre>ara trim list --help\nara trim derive --help\nara trim apply --help\nara trim release --help</pre>\n<h3>Temper</h3>\n<p>A Temper contains reviewed control candidates from Tailor and Needle runs.</p>\n<p>Tailor prepares the review context.</p>\n<p>Needle proposes changes through the Temper channel interface.</p>\n<p>Candidates remain pending until an operator selects and applies them.</p>\n<p>1. Open Temper in Armory.</p>\n<p>2. Select the target armor.</p>\n<p>3. Supply the source materials and control intent.</p>\n<p>4. Start the Tailor run.</p>\n<p>5. Examine each candidate and its destination.</p>\n<p>6. Select the accepted candidates.</p>\n<p>7. Apply the selection to the armor.</p>\n<p>8. Examine the active policy and instructions.</p>\n<p>Temper can supply Bulwark controls and ABSOLUTE instructions.</p>\n<p>Trim and Temper use separate armor slots.</p>')]),
    DocTopic(slug='absolute', title='ABSOLUTE.md', rarity='rare', tier_roman='III', sigil='<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M6 3 H18 A2 2 0 0 1 20 5 V19 A2 2 0 0 1 18 21 H6 Z"/><path d="M8 7 H16 M8 11 H16 M8 15 H13"/></svg>', blurb='System instructions for supported model requests.', abilities=[], sections=[DocSection("Product guide", '<p>ARA uses a supervisor process, a model proxy, runtime guards, and an operator interface.</p>\n<p>Each supervised session has an identifier, state files, and an IPC connection.</p>\n<p>IPC means interprocess communication.</p>\n<h3>Control path</h3>\n<p>1. The launcher prepares the worker environment.</p>\n<p>2. The supervisor creates the session socket and token.</p>\n<p>3. The runtime guard sends operation queries to the supervisor.</p>\n<p>4. The supervisor evaluates the active policy.</p>\n<p>5. The guard applies the returned decision.</p>\n<p>6. The audit system records the decision.</p>\n<p>The supervisor can return <code>allow</code>, <code>deny</code>, or <code>approval</code>.</p>\n<p>An approval request waits for an operator decision where the interception path supports that action.</p>\n<h3>Policy components</h3>\n<p>Bulwark stores resource rules in <code>bulwark.md</code> under the state directory.</p>\n<p>WALLS, Rhema, and override compatibility modules project these rules into existing interfaces.</p>\n<p>ABSOLUTE supplies the active armor instructions to supported model request envelopes.</p>\n<p>Trim and Temper can add instructions and policy sections to the active equipment.</p>\n<p>The native guard intercepts supported libc operations.</p>\n<p>Node hooks intercept supported JavaScript operations.</p>\n<p>Linux seccomp notification provides an additional interception path for selected network operations.</p>\n<p>These controls operate at different boundaries.</p>\n<p>The available control path depends on the host system and worker runtime.</p>\n<h3>Model path</h3>\n<p>The launcher configures the worker to use the model proxy.</p>\n<p>The proxy inspects supported request and reply formats.</p>\n<p>It evaluates the relevant Bulwark rules before it forwards traffic.</p>\n<p>It can insert ABSOLUTE instructions or return a refusal response.</p>\n<p>A shadow request evaluates an alternate instruction set without replacing the live reply.</p>\n<h3>Source map</h3>\n<p>| Component | Source |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| Command parser | <code>src/ara/cli.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Worker launch | <code>src/ara/launch.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Environment preparation | <code>src/ara/containment.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Supervisor and IPC | <code>src/ara/supervisor.py</code>, <code>src/ara/ipc.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Rule parser | <code>src/ara/bulwark.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Model proxy | <code>src/ara/mitm_proxy.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Native guard | <code>src/guard/libc_shim/</code> |</p>\n<p>| Node hooks | <code>src/guard/</code> |</p>\n<p>| Operator web server | <code>src/ara/armory/app.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Equipment | <code>src/ara/armors.py</code>, <code>src/ara/weapons.py</code>, <code>src/ara/trims.py</code>, <code>src/ara/tempers.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Forge | <code>src/ara/forge/</code> |</p>\n<p>| Task state | <code>src/ara/task_session.py</code>, <code>src/ara/task_repair.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Runtime channels | <code>src/ara/runtime/</code> |</p>\n<p>See the policy reference and integration guide.</p>')]),
    DocTopic(slug='rhema', title='Rhema', rarity='epic', tier_roman='IV', sigil='<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M2 12 S5 5 12 5 S22 12 22 12 S19 19 12 19 S2 12 2 12 Z"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"/></svg>', blurb='Inspection of model requests and replies.', abilities=[], sections=[DocSection("Product guide", '<p>ARA uses a supervisor process, a model proxy, runtime guards, and an operator interface.</p>\n<p>Each supervised session has an identifier, state files, and an IPC connection.</p>\n<p>IPC means interprocess communication.</p>\n<h3>Control path</h3>\n<p>1. The launcher prepares the worker environment.</p>\n<p>2. The supervisor creates the session socket and token.</p>\n<p>3. The runtime guard sends operation queries to the supervisor.</p>\n<p>4. The supervisor evaluates the active policy.</p>\n<p>5. The guard applies the returned decision.</p>\n<p>6. The audit system records the decision.</p>\n<p>The supervisor can return <code>allow</code>, <code>deny</code>, or <code>approval</code>.</p>\n<p>An approval request waits for an operator decision where the interception path supports that action.</p>\n<h3>Policy components</h3>\n<p>Bulwark stores resource rules in <code>bulwark.md</code> under the state directory.</p>\n<p>WALLS, Rhema, and override compatibility modules project these rules into existing interfaces.</p>\n<p>ABSOLUTE supplies the active armor instructions to supported model request envelopes.</p>\n<p>Trim and Temper can add instructions and policy sections to the active equipment.</p>\n<p>The native guard intercepts supported libc operations.</p>\n<p>Node hooks intercept supported JavaScript operations.</p>\n<p>Linux seccomp notification provides an additional interception path for selected network operations.</p>\n<p>These controls operate at different boundaries.</p>\n<p>The available control path depends on the host system and worker runtime.</p>\n<h3>Model path</h3>\n<p>The launcher configures the worker to use the model proxy.</p>\n<p>The proxy inspects supported request and reply formats.</p>\n<p>It evaluates the relevant Bulwark rules before it forwards traffic.</p>\n<p>It can insert ABSOLUTE instructions or return a refusal response.</p>\n<p>A shadow request evaluates an alternate instruction set without replacing the live reply.</p>\n<h3>Source map</h3>\n<p>| Component | Source |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| Command parser | <code>src/ara/cli.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Worker launch | <code>src/ara/launch.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Environment preparation | <code>src/ara/containment.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Supervisor and IPC | <code>src/ara/supervisor.py</code>, <code>src/ara/ipc.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Rule parser | <code>src/ara/bulwark.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Model proxy | <code>src/ara/mitm_proxy.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Native guard | <code>src/guard/libc_shim/</code> |</p>\n<p>| Node hooks | <code>src/guard/</code> |</p>\n<p>| Operator web server | <code>src/ara/armory/app.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Equipment | <code>src/ara/armors.py</code>, <code>src/ara/weapons.py</code>, <code>src/ara/trims.py</code>, <code>src/ara/tempers.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Forge | <code>src/ara/forge/</code> |</p>\n<p>| Task state | <code>src/ara/task_session.py</code>, <code>src/ara/task_repair.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Runtime channels | <code>src/ara/runtime/</code> |</p>\n<p>See the policy reference and integration guide.</p>')]),
    DocTopic(slug='overrides-blackholes', title='Overrides & Blackholes', rarity='rare', tier_roman='III', sigil='<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="8" cy="12" r="4"/><path d="M12 12 H21 M17 12 V16 M21 12 V15"/></svg>', blurb='Persistent rule entries in Bulwark.', abilities=[], sections=[DocSection("Product guide", '<p>Bulwark stores rules in <code>&lt;state-root&gt;/bulwark.md</code>.</p>\n<p>A rule selects an action, a layer, and a match expression.</p>\n<p>Section headers select the sessions to which a rule applies.</p>\n<h3>Grammar</h3>\n<pre>&lt;action&gt; [&lt;modifier&gt; ...] &lt;layer&gt;[:&lt;sublayer&gt;] &lt;match&gt; [# comment]</pre>\n<pre>## global\ndeny fs **/.ssh/**\napproval exec /usr/bin/curl\nallow fs /workspace/**\n\n## armor: research\ndeny net blocked.example.com\napproval x3 then:deny ask &quot;restricted material&quot;\n\n## config\ncounter_exhaust_default: deny</pre>\n<h3>Actions</h3>\n<p>| Action | Meaning |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| <code>deny</code> | Stop the matching operation |</p>\n<p>| <code>approval</code> | Request an operator decision |</p>\n<p>| <code>allow</code> | Permit the matching operation |</p>\n<p>| <code>rewrite</code> | Describe a request rewrite |</p>\n<p>| <code>shadow_rewrite</code> | Describe an alternate request for comparison |</p>\n<p>The deterministic evaluator selects <code>deny</code>, <code>approval</code>, and <code>allow</code> rules.</p>\n<p>The parser also accepts rewrite actions.</p>\n<p>ABSOLUTE projection and the proxy control the live rewrite path.</p>\n<h3>Layers</h3>\n<p>| Layer | Match expression |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| <code>fs</code> | File path |</p>\n<p>| <code>nav</code> | Directory path |</p>\n<p>| <code>perm</code> | Optional <code>op=&lt;operation&gt;</code>, then a path |</p>\n<p>| <code>exec</code> | Optional <code>argv=&lt;pattern&gt;</code>, then an executable path |</p>\n<p>| <code>net</code> | Host, with an optional port |</p>\n<p>| <code>url</code> | Optional HTTP method, then a URL |</p>\n<p>| <code>env</code> | Environment variable name |</p>\n<p>| <code>ask</code> | Text in a model request |</p>\n<p>| <code>reply</code> | Text in a model reply |</p>\n<p>| <code>acp</code> | Agent communication method name |</p>\n<p>| <code>oc:&lt;tool&gt;</code> | Target of a named worker tool |</p>\n<p><code>oc</code> selects the complete worker tool family.</p>\n<p>Common tool names include <code>read</code>, <code>write</code>, <code>edit</code>, <code>bash</code>, <code>glob</code>, <code>grep</code>, <code>webfetch</code>, and <code>websearch</code>.</p>\n<p>A worker integration must send tool queries for these rules to operate.</p>\n<h3>Scope</h3>\n<p>| Header | Application |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| <code>## global</code> | All sessions |</p>\n<p>| <code>## armor: research</code> | Sessions with the research armor |</p>\n<p>| <code>## armor: alpha+beta</code> | Sessions with either named armor |</p>\n<p>| <code>## armor: *-research</code> | All armors except research |</p>\n<p>| <code>## global -armor: research</code> | Global scope with the named exclusion |</p>\n<h3>Precedence</h3>\n<p>Bulwark evaluates these groups in order:</p>\n<p>1. Global deny rules.</p>\n<p>2. Armor deny rules.</p>\n<p>3. Global approval rules.</p>\n<p>4. Armor approval rules.</p>\n<p>5. Global allow rules.</p>\n<p>6. Armor allow rules.</p>\n<p>7. The default decision.</p>\n<p>File order selects the first applicable rule within a group.</p>\n<p>The default evaluator decision is <code>allow</code> when no rule matches.</p>\n<p>Individual interception paths can apply additional restrictions.</p>\n<p>A broad allow rule does not cancel a matching deny rule.</p>\n<h3>Patterns and modifiers</h3>\n<p><code>*</code> matches characters within one path segment.</p>\n<p><code>**</code> matches across path segments.</p>\n<p><code>?</code> matches one character within a segment.</p>\n<p>The <code>regex:</code> prefix selects a regular expression.</p>\n<p>| Modifier | Function |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| <code>ttl 30s</code> | Expire the rule after thirty seconds |</p>\n<p>| <code>ttl 15m</code> | Expire the rule after fifteen minutes |</p>\n<p>| <code>ttl 2h</code> | Expire the rule after two hours |</p>\n<p>| <code>ttl 7d</code> | Expire the rule after seven days |</p>\n<p>| <code>x3</code> | Set a maximum of three matches |</p>\n<p>| <code>then:deny</code> | Deny after the match count expires |</p>\n<p>| <code>then:allow</code> | Permit after the match count expires |</p>\n<p>| <code>then:approval</code> | Request approval after the match count expires |</p>\n<p>| <code>then:fallthrough</code> | Continue to the next rule after the match count expires |</p>\n<p>| <code>highlight</code> | Mark the rule for prominent display |</p>\n<h3>Verify policy</h3>\n<p>1. Edit <code>bulwark.md</code> in the state directory.</p>\n<p>2. Examine parser errors.</p>\n<p>   ```sh</p>\n<p>   ara bulwark verify --state-root .ara</p>\n<p>   ```</p>\n<p>3. Examine the resulting rule list.</p>\n<p>   ```sh</p>\n<p>   ara bulwark list --state-root .ara</p>\n<p>   ```</p>\n<p>Use <code>ara bulwark migrate --dry-run</code> to examine migration from legacy policy files.</p>\n<p>Use the same state directory for the migration and the supervised sessions.</p>')]),
    DocTopic(slug='approvals', title='Approvals', rarity='uncommon', tier_roman='II', sigil='<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="5" y="11" width="14" height="10" rx="2"/><path d="M8 11 V7 A4 4 0 0 1 16 7 V11"/></svg>', blurb='Operator decisions for pending operations.', abilities=[], sections=[DocSection("Product guide", '<h3>Start a supervised session</h3>\n<p>1. Start Armory with the intended state directory.</p>\n<p>2. Open the Armors page.</p>\n<p>3. Select the armor for the task.</p>\n<p>4. Examine its Bulwark rules.</p>\n<p>5. Select its ABSOLUTE instructions.</p>\n<p>6. Start the worker with the same state directory.</p>\n<pre>ara launch --state-root .ara --contain --contain-profile hermes-gateway -- hermes gateway run</pre>\n<p>The worker executable must already be installed.</p>\n<p>The <code>--contain</code> option prepares separate session directories for worker configuration and temporary files.</p>\n<h3>Select equipment</h3>\n<p>1. Open Armory.</p>\n<p>2. Select an armor.</p>\n<p>3. Select a sealed weapon for the task method.</p>\n<p>4. Apply a Trim for engagement context.</p>\n<p>5. Apply a reviewed Temper when additional controls are necessary.</p>\n<p>6. Examine the effective policy before the run.</p>\n<p>An armor can have an active Trim and an active Temper at the same time.</p>\n<p>See Equipment for the file structure and lifecycle.</p>\n<h3>Respond to an approval request</h3>\n<p>1. Open the Approvals page.</p>\n<p>2. Examine the requested operation and its target.</p>\n<p>3. Select the decision scope.</p>\n<p>4. Enter the reason.</p>\n<p>5. Submit the decision.</p>\n<p>The command interface provides the same approval actions:</p>\n<pre>ara approval list --state-root .ara\nara approval show APPROVAL_ID --state-root .ara\nara approval respond APPROVAL_ID deny --reason &quot;Outside task scope&quot; --state-root .ara</pre>\n<p>Replace <code>APPROVAL_ID</code> with the identifier from the list.</p>\n<p><code>allow-session</code> and <code>deny-session</code> apply to the session.</p>\n<p><code>allow-always</code> and <code>deny-always</code> create persistent entries.</p>\n<p>The <code>--ttl</code> option adds an expiry to persistent entries.</p>\n<h3>Examine a run</h3>\n<p>1. Open Courtyard to examine current sessions.</p>\n<p>2. Open the task detail to examine its current chunk.</p>\n<p>3. Examine the evidence and channel events.</p>\n<p>4. Examine unresolved findings and leads.</p>\n<p>5. Use the task controls to pause or stop the run.</p>\n<p>Use the following commands for session and audit status:</p>\n<pre>ara status --state-root .ara\nara session list --state-root .ara\nara verify-audit all --state-root .ara</pre>')]),
    DocTopic(slug='audit', title='Audit', rarity='rare', tier_roman='III', sigil='<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 7 H7 A4 4 0 0 0 7 15 H9"/><path d="M15 7 H17 A4 4 0 0 1 17 15 H15"/><path d="M8 11 H16"/></svg>', blurb='Policy decisions and integrity checks.', abilities=[], sections=[DocSection("Product guide", '<p>ARA records observations separately from claims and operator decisions.</p>\n<p>Evidence records identify captured artifacts and their content hashes.</p>\n<p>Findings and leads refer to these records through structured channels.</p>\n<h3>Evidence</h3>\n<p>An evidence record connects a file to its session, tool, action, and content hash.</p>\n<p>Verification reads the current file and compares its hash with the recorded value.</p>\n<p>A changed file fails that comparison.</p>\n<pre>ara evidence record --help\nara evidence list --help\nara evidence verify --help</pre>\n<p>Keep evidence files at their recorded locations when verification is necessary.</p>\n<h3>Findings</h3>\n<p>A finding describes a supported result.</p>\n<p>The finding channel checks the submitted fields and evidence references.</p>\n<p>The validator can check evidence freshness and claim markers.</p>\n<p>Operator sign-off uses the finding&#x27;s current review state and validation result.</p>\n<p>A finding can remain a draft while evidence or review is incomplete.</p>\n<p>Examine the validation response before you submit a correction.</p>\n<h3>Leads</h3>\n<p>A lead identifies a question for further investigation.</p>\n<p>A lead can refer to evidence or an existing fact.</p>\n<p>The lead lifecycle preserves the investigation state and its origin.</p>\n<p>A lead does not become a confirmed finding merely because it exists.</p>\n<h3>Anomalies and facts</h3>\n<p>An anomaly records an unexpected observation.</p>\n<p>Facts provide structured observations for later channel operations.</p>\n<p>Runtime channels use these records to select further work and preserve context.</p>\n<h3>Audit chain</h3>\n<p>Policy decisions are written to session audit records.</p>\n<p>The hash chain detects changes to the recorded sequence.</p>\n<p>1. List the sessions.</p>\n<p>   ```sh</p>\n<p>   ara session list --state-root .ara</p>\n<p>   ```</p>\n<p>2. Verify the audit chains.</p>\n<p>   ```sh</p>\n<p>   ara verify-audit all --state-root .ara</p>\n<p>   ```</p>\n<p>3. Examine a reported mismatch before you use the affected sequence.</p>\n<h3>Baselines and canaries</h3>\n<p>A baseline records a reference resource set for comparison.</p>\n<p>A canary is a marked value or file used to detect access or disclosure.</p>\n<p>Canary observation and reply blocking use distinct control paths.</p>\n<p>A matching reply rule supplies the blocking decision.</p>\n<pre>ara baseline --help\nara canary --help</pre>')]),
    DocTopic(slug='sessions', title='Sessions', rarity='uncommon', tier_roman='II', sigil='<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 12 H7 L9 6 L13 18 L15 12 H21"/></svg>', blurb='Worker execution and task progress.', abilities=[], sections=[DocSection("Product guide", '<p>A task run connects an objective, equipment, a worker session, and persistent progress.</p>\n<p>Hermes provides the persistent task execution path.</p>\n<p>Armory presents the task state and operator controls.</p>\n<h3>Preparation</h3>\n<p>1. Select the task objective.</p>\n<p>2. Select an armor and sealed weapon.</p>\n<p>3. Apply the engagement Trim when necessary.</p>\n<p>4. Examine the task preflight results.</p>\n<p>5. Start the run from Armory.</p>\n<p>Preflight examines the necessary equipment and runtime configuration.</p>\n<p>The task controller creates the run records and worker session.</p>\n<h3>Chunks and branches</h3>\n<p>A chunk is a bounded procedure within the equipped weapon.</p>\n<p>It defines the available channel surface and progress conditions.</p>\n<p>A branch records the next procedure path.</p>\n<p>The runtime checks a requested transition against the current chunk state.</p>\n<p>An unresolved prerequisite can stop a transition.</p>\n<p>The task can also stop for an operator decision, an evidence requirement, or a time limit.</p>\n<p>The task detail shows the active chunk and recorded events.</p>\n<h3>Repair</h3>\n<p>The repair path records supported state changes through explicit channel operations.</p>\n<p>It can complete a pending commit or repair a chunk transition.</p>\n<p>The controller checks the current run state before it accepts the change.</p>\n<p>1. Examine the task detail and recent channel errors.</p>\n<p>2. Examine the evidence referenced by the current chunk.</p>\n<p>3. Resolve the missing prerequisite through its channel.</p>\n<p>4. Use the task recovery control when the prerequisite is complete.</p>\n<p>5. Examine the next task event.</p>\n<p>Use <code>ara debug-log --help</code> to select task or session filters.</p>\n<p>Use <code>ara task-extend --help</code> for a task time extension.</p>\n<h3>Completion</h3>\n<p>A completed run retains its task records, evidence references, findings, and audit history.</p>\n<p>A stopped run retains its recorded progress.</p>\n<p>Operator controls determine whether a run resumes or ends.</p>\n<p>See Evidence and Integrations.</p>')]),
    DocTopic(slug='guard', title='Guard Runtime', rarity='rare', tier_roman='III', sigil='<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="3"/><path d="M12 2 V5 M12 19 V22 M2 12 H5 M19 12 H22 M4.9 4.9 L7 7 M17 17 L19.1 19.1 M4.9 19.1 L7 17 M17 7 L19.1 4.9"/></svg>', blurb='Runtime interception of supported operations.', abilities=[], sections=[DocSection("Product guide", '<p>ARA uses a supervisor process, a model proxy, runtime guards, and an operator interface.</p>\n<p>Each supervised session has an identifier, state files, and an IPC connection.</p>\n<p>IPC means interprocess communication.</p>\n<h3>Control path</h3>\n<p>1. The launcher prepares the worker environment.</p>\n<p>2. The supervisor creates the session socket and token.</p>\n<p>3. The runtime guard sends operation queries to the supervisor.</p>\n<p>4. The supervisor evaluates the active policy.</p>\n<p>5. The guard applies the returned decision.</p>\n<p>6. The audit system records the decision.</p>\n<p>The supervisor can return <code>allow</code>, <code>deny</code>, or <code>approval</code>.</p>\n<p>An approval request waits for an operator decision where the interception path supports that action.</p>\n<h3>Policy components</h3>\n<p>Bulwark stores resource rules in <code>bulwark.md</code> under the state directory.</p>\n<p>WALLS, Rhema, and override compatibility modules project these rules into existing interfaces.</p>\n<p>ABSOLUTE supplies the active armor instructions to supported model request envelopes.</p>\n<p>Trim and Temper can add instructions and policy sections to the active equipment.</p>\n<p>The native guard intercepts supported libc operations.</p>\n<p>Node hooks intercept supported JavaScript operations.</p>\n<p>Linux seccomp notification provides an additional interception path for selected network operations.</p>\n<p>These controls operate at different boundaries.</p>\n<p>The available control path depends on the host system and worker runtime.</p>\n<h3>Model path</h3>\n<p>The launcher configures the worker to use the model proxy.</p>\n<p>The proxy inspects supported request and reply formats.</p>\n<p>It evaluates the relevant Bulwark rules before it forwards traffic.</p>\n<p>It can insert ABSOLUTE instructions or return a refusal response.</p>\n<p>A shadow request evaluates an alternate instruction set without replacing the live reply.</p>\n<h3>Source map</h3>\n<p>| Component | Source |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| Command parser | <code>src/ara/cli.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Worker launch | <code>src/ara/launch.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Environment preparation | <code>src/ara/containment.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Supervisor and IPC | <code>src/ara/supervisor.py</code>, <code>src/ara/ipc.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Rule parser | <code>src/ara/bulwark.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Model proxy | <code>src/ara/mitm_proxy.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Native guard | <code>src/guard/libc_shim/</code> |</p>\n<p>| Node hooks | <code>src/guard/</code> |</p>\n<p>| Operator web server | <code>src/ara/armory/app.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Equipment | <code>src/ara/armors.py</code>, <code>src/ara/weapons.py</code>, <code>src/ara/trims.py</code>, <code>src/ara/tempers.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Forge | <code>src/ara/forge/</code> |</p>\n<p>| Task state | <code>src/ara/task_session.py</code>, <code>src/ara/task_repair.py</code> |</p>\n<p>| Runtime channels | <code>src/ara/runtime/</code> |</p>\n<p>See the policy reference and integration guide.</p>')]),
    DocTopic(slug='mitm', title='TLS MITM Proxy', rarity='legendary', tier_roman='V', sigil='<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 12 H21"/><path d="M7 8 L3 12 L7 16"/><path d="M17 8 L21 12 L17 16"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="2"/></svg>', blurb='Model traffic through the ARA proxy.', abilities=[], sections=[DocSection("Product guide", '<h3>State directory</h3>\n<p>Use one state directory for Armory and the workers that it supervises.</p>\n<p>The <code>--state-root</code> option selects the directory for commands that provide this option.</p>\n<p>Commands otherwise use <code>ARA_STATE_ROOT</code> where supported, then <code>.ara</code> in the current directory.</p>\n<pre>export ARA_STATE_ROOT=&quot;$PWD/.ara&quot;\nara armory --state-root &quot;$ARA_STATE_ROOT&quot;</pre>\n<p>The state directory contains policy, equipment, sessions, approvals, certificates, and audit records.</p>\n<p>Back up this directory with its access permissions.</p>\n<p>Keep the state directory outside version control.</p>\n<h3>Runtime options</h3>\n<p>| Variable | Function |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| <code>ARA_PYTHON</code> | Select the Python interpreter for <code>bin/ara</code> |</p>\n<p>| <code>ARA_STATE_ROOT</code> | Select the shared state directory |</p>\n<p>| <code>ARA_OPENCLAW_BIN</code> | Select the OpenClaw executable |</p>\n<p>| <code>ARA_CLAUDE_BIN</code> | Select the Claude Code executable |</p>\n<p>| <code>ARA_HEARTBEAT_TIMEOUT_S</code> | Set the supervisor heartbeat timeout in seconds |</p>\n<p>| <code>ARA_APPROVAL_TIMEOUT_S</code> | Set the model proxy approval timeout |</p>\n<p>| <code>ARA_SKIP_MITM</code> | Disable the model proxy when set to a nonzero value |</p>\n<p>| <code>ARA_CHUNK_INHIBITION_ENFORCE</code> | Activate chunk inhibition enforcement |</p>\n<p>The launcher supplies <code>ARA_SOCKET</code> and <code>ARA_SESSION_TOKEN</code> to contained workers.</p>\n<p>Use these values through the supplied integrations.</p>\n<h3>Armory network settings</h3>\n<p>The default address is <code>127.0.0.1</code>.</p>\n<p>The default port is <code>4770</code>.</p>\n<p>Use <code>--bind</code> to select an address.</p>\n<p>Use <code>--port</code> to select a port.</p>\n<p>Use <code>--tls-cert</code> and <code>--tls-key</code> to supply a TLS certificate and private key.</p>\n<p>Use <code>--behind-proxy</code> for a deployment behind a reverse proxy.</p>\n<p>Use <code>ara armory --help</code> to examine the complete option list.</p>\n<h3>Model certificates</h3>\n<p>The model proxy uses OpenSSL to create its certificate authority and host certificates.</p>\n<p>Contained workers receive the certificate configuration from the launcher.</p>\n<p>Keep the certificate authority key in the protected state directory.</p>\n<p>Use the session logs to diagnose certificate trust failures.</p>\n<h3>Native guard</h3>\n<p>Build the guard from the source checkout:</p>\n<pre>make -C src/guard/libc_shim</pre>\n<p>The output is <code>build/libara_shim.so</code>.</p>\n<p>Linux uses <code>LD_PRELOAD</code> for this guard.</p>\n<p>macOS uses <code>DYLD_INSERT_LIBRARIES</code> where the process permits library injection.</p>\n<p>Host restrictions can prevent library injection into protected processes.</p>')]),
    DocTopic(slug='cli', title='CLI Reference', rarity='common', tier_roman='I', sigil='<svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 3 H15 V8 L19 18 A2 2 0 0 1 17 21 H7 A2 2 0 0 1 5 18 L9 8 Z"/><path d="M7 14 H17"/></svg>', blurb='Commands and integration interfaces.', abilities=[], sections=[DocSection("Product guide", '<h3>Command groups</h3>\n<p>Use <code>ara --help</code> for all commands.</p>\n<p>Use <code>ara COMMAND --help</code> for a command&#x27;s arguments.</p>\n<p>| Group | Function |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| <code>launch</code>, <code>claude</code>, <code>openclaw</code> | Start supervised workers |</p>\n<p>| <code>armory</code> | Start the operator interface |</p>\n<p>| <code>armor</code>, <code>weapon</code>, <code>trim</code> | Manage equipment |</p>\n<p>| <code>forge</code> | Create weapons and packs |</p>\n<p>| <code>bulwark</code> | Verify and inspect policy |</p>\n<p>| <code>approval</code> | Examine and resolve approval requests |</p>\n<p>| <code>overrides</code>, <code>blackholes</code> | Manage compatibility policy entries |</p>\n<p>| <code>session</code>, <code>status</code> | Examine session state |</p>\n<p>| <code>evidence</code>, <code>verify-audit</code> | Examine evidence and audit integrity |</p>\n<p>| <code>canary</code>, <code>baseline</code> | Manage observation aids |</p>\n<p>| <code>engagement</code> | Examine engagement state |</p>\n<p>| <code>tool</code> | Manage tool classification |</p>\n<p>| <code>debug-log</code> | Read structured debug events |</p>\n<h3>Armory routes</h3>\n<p>Armory serves HTML pages through its HTTP server.</p>\n<p>The token parameter <code>t</code> authenticates normal browser requests.</p>\n<p>Form actions use the application&#x27;s supplied controls and redirect responses.</p>\n<p>| Route | Page |</p>\n<p>|---|---|</p>\n<p>| <code>/healthz</code> | Health response |</p>\n<p>| <code>/status</code> | Equipment and session status |</p>\n<p>| <code>/courtyard</code> | Current activity |</p>\n<p>| <code>/armors</code> | Armor catalog |</p>\n<p>| <code>/weapons</code> | Weapon catalog |</p>\n<p>| <code>/forge</code> | Forge sessions |</p>\n<p>| <code>/bulwark</code> | Policy rules |</p>\n<p>| <code>/rhema</code> | Model traffic |</p>\n<p>| <code>/approvals</code> | Approval requests |</p>\n<p>| <code>/audit</code> | Policy decisions |</p>\n<p>| <code>/baselines</code> | Baseline records |</p>\n<p>| <code>/graph</code> | Relationships |</p>\n<p>| <code>/docs</code> | Product topics |</p>\n<p>The server defines route handling in <code>src/ara/armory/app.py</code>.</p>\n<h3>IPC tool queries</h3>\n<p>Contained workers receive <code>ARA_SOCKET</code> and <code>ARA_SESSION_TOKEN</code>.</p>\n<p>The socket uses newline-delimited JSON messages.</p>\n<p>Use the supplied IPC client for the session authentication handshake.</p>\n<p>Send the tool query before the worker dispatches the operation.</p>\n<pre>{\n  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;query&quot;,\n  &quot;seq&quot;: 1,\n  &quot;op&quot;: &quot;oc.tool_call&quot;,\n  &quot;tool&quot;: &quot;read&quot;,\n  &quot;target&quot;: &quot;/workspace/example.txt&quot;,\n  &quot;pid&quot;: 1234\n}</pre>\n<p>The supervisor returns a decision with the matching <code>seq</code> value.</p>\n<pre>{\n  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;decision&quot;,\n  &quot;seq&quot;: 1,\n  &quot;verdict&quot;: &quot;deny&quot;,\n  &quot;reason&quot;: &quot;Matched policy rule&quot;,\n  &quot;rule_id&quot;: &quot;example-rule&quot;,\n  &quot;touched&quot;: &quot;/workspace/example.txt&quot;\n}</pre>\n<p>1. Wait for the matching decision.</p>\n<p>2. For <code>allow</code>, dispatch the operation.</p>\n<p>3. For <code>deny</code>, return an operation error.</p>\n<p>4. For <code>approval</code>, wait for the resolved decision.</p>\n<p>5. Record the result through the appropriate runtime channel.</p>\n<p>The IPC implementation is in <code>src/ara/ipc.py</code> and <code>src/guard/ipc.mjs</code>.</p>')]),
]


def by_slug(slug: str) -> DocTopic | None:
    for t in TOPICS:
        if t.slug == slug:
            return t
    return None
